import "../../../../sass/accessibility.scss";
import { urls } from "../../../../data";

<div class="a11y-pattern-content">
  <h1>Menu Button Accessibility</h1>
  <h2>Best Practices</h2>
  <p>
    The trigger <strong>must</strong> be a real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>
    element and the first child of the menu button, with the dropdown after it.
    Its accessible name comes from its text, or from <code>aria-label</code>
    when it shows only an icon.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trigger <strong>must</strong> set <code>aria-expanded</code> to
    <code>"true"</code> when the dropdown is open and <code>"false"</code> when
    it is closed. The dropdown is shown only while the trigger is expanded. The
    trigger <strong>must</strong> set <code>aria-controls</code> to the id of
    the element it opens.
  </p>
  <p>What the trigger announces depends on what it opens:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      For an <a href=`${urls.components}/action-menu`>action menu</a> or a
      <a href=`${urls.components}/filter-menu`>filter menu</a> , set
      <code>aria-haspopup="menu"</code> , and point the menu at the trigger with
      <code>aria-labelledby</code> .
    </li>
    <li>
      For a <a href=`${urls.components}/navigation-menu`>navigation menu</a> ,
      set no <code>aria-haspopup</code> . The dropdown holds plain links, not an
      ARIA menu.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A filter chip trigger <strong>must</strong> state in its text when a filter
    is applied, visibly or with visually hidden text. It
    <strong>must not</strong> use <code>aria-pressed</code> , because pressing
    it opens a menu instead of toggling the filter.
  </p>
  <h2>Interaction Design</h2>
  <p>
    This section provides interaction design for keyboard and screen reader
    users.
  </p>
  <h3>Keyboard</h3>
  <p>
    <code>ENTER</code> or <code>SPACEBAR</code> on the trigger opens the
    dropdown and moves focus to the first enabled item. In an action or filter
    menu, the arrow keys move between items; in a navigation menu, the
    <code>TAB</code> key does.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>ESCAPE</code> closes the dropdown and returns focus to the trigger.
    Moving focus or clicking outside the menu button closes it without moving
    focus back.
  </p>
  <h3>Dismissal</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Action menu: activating an enabled item closes the dropdown, including a
      checkbox or radio action.
    </li>
    <li>
      Filter menu: selecting a radio item closes it; toggling a checkbox item
      leaves it open; an Apply button closes it.
    </li>
    <li>Navigation menu: activating a link or button closes it.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Screen Reader</h3>
  <p>
    The trigger <strong>must</strong> announce as a button with its expanded or
    collapsed state. A trigger for an action or filter menu also announces that
    it opens a menu.
  </p>
</div>
